-- Q14373: Nontaxable Dispositions [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1033 and Reg. Section 1.1033(a)-2(c)(8), severance damages compensate the property owner for the decline in fair market value of retained property caused by the government''s taking. These damages reduce the basis of the retained property rather than being treated as proceeds from the converted portion.
Wrong (A): Compensation for the property actually taken is the condemnation award itself, not severance damages. Severance damages address harm to property the owner still holds.
Wrong (C): Severance damages are not a penalty for delay. They represent the economic harm to the retained property resulting from the partial taking.
Wrong (D): Severance damages are not legal fee reimbursements. Legal fees are a separate category of condemnation-related costs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14373;

-- Q14381: Nontaxable Dispositions [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1033(a)(2), Mendez realizes $200,000 gain ($500,000 minus $300,000 basis) but reinvests only $450,000, so $50,000 is recognized and $150,000 is deferred. Total replacement basis is $300,000 ($450,000 minus $150,000 deferred gain), allocated proportionally: Building A gets $233,333 and Building B gets $66,667.
Wrong (A): Using cost as basis ignores the Section 1033 requirement to reduce replacement property basis by deferred gain to preserve it for future recognition.
Wrong (C): This incorrectly allocates basis without proper proportional reduction for the $150,000 of deferred gain across both properties.
Wrong (D): This understates Building A''s basis by applying an incorrect deferred gain allocation formula that does not reflect relative cost proportions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14381;

-- Q14384: Nontaxable Dispositions [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 453 and the installment sale regulations, in a wrap-around mortgage sale the seller remains liable on the existing mortgage. The full $600,000 selling price is the contract price. Gross profit is $350,000 ($600,000 minus $250,000 basis), yielding 58.33% ($350,000 / $600,000).
Wrong (B): 43.75% results from incorrectly reducing the contract price by the $200,000 existing mortgage, which applies only when the buyer assumes the mortgage under Treas. Reg. Section 15A.453-1(b)(2)(iii).
Wrong (C): 87.50% results from dividing gross profit by a reduced contract price, combining both errors.
Wrong (D): 35.00% does not follow the wrap-around installment sale formula under Section 453.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14384;

-- Q14391: Nontaxable Dispositions [hard/L3]
